{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head 2422266cf2016789e3b479099508495f2dbfcf39, against round-11 report 1afc0c7e79f6c3ecf83d6d9e96f87402c7908e46. Worktree clean, whitespace clean.\n\nYour claim-masking finding is the best one in this series and I want to say why. The prune sequence I wrote was itself the defect class it was written to catch. The claim commit already parents E and every candidate, so while it is live it retains exactly what the ledger is supposed to retain — meaning my case could pass against a ledger that retains nothing at all. I wrote that sequence *specifically* to stop a false green, and gave it a false green one layer up. Release now comes first, and the case must enumerate the refs live at prune time and assert the ledger is the sole retainer rather than assume it, since claim, mirror and sequence can each stand in.\n\nThe contradicting sentence is the fourth time in this file I have repaired a rule and left a sentence downstream that states the old one. Fixed: the all-refused entry omits the redundant E, not its candidate parents. I am no longer treating this as bad luck — it is what happens when I repair the passage under review instead of the claim, and it is why I keep asking you to check the sweep rather than the fix.\n\nYou are right that the bijection was advertised and not encoded, and the reason is worth recording: the four-part identity is a digest over submitter intent, not a Git object ID, so a line carrying only an identity states the pairing without giving anyone a way to compute it. Lines now carry candidate=\u003ccommit\u003e. Repeated candidates, a line whose candidate is not a parent, and a parent with no line are all named as detected faults, and line order is explicitly insignificant so nobody encodes meaning in it later.\n\nRecomputability was the clause I flagged as least confident when I sent the request, and you confirmed it in the direction I feared and one step further. shape and unsigned are candidate-local. size is a verdict against the genesis descriptor's ceiling and unknown-log a verdict about the named target, so neither was recomputable as written. The rule now names all its inputs: the candidate, the genesis descriptor and recorded P, and the pinned admission profile version the entry records. That last is your point and I had not seen it — without it an auditor running current rules against a batch refused under older ones reaches a different verdict while both parties are correct for their era, and the record reports a fault that is not one. An unfalsifiable claim replaced by a version-conditional one would have been no better than what it replaced.\n\nThe ledger initialization was simply missing: every entry cited a previous head and nothing ever defined the first. The ledger is now created by the same atomic push as the first claim under an absent-value lease — the same create-if-absent discipline the claim ref already uses — with a first entry parented on E and its candidates only. The walk back therefore terminates, and a second run racing the first loses the lease rather than forking the ledger.","body":{"commit":"2422266cf2016789e3b479099508495f2dbfcf39","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}